median ratio of 0.990 on $N=32$ and 19/32 wins.  This row has no ASLS branch
and no enlarged operator search; it is the compact strict-linear bank selected
by internal five-fold validation.

The selected AOM-PLS regression variant is \texttt{ASLS-AOM-compact-cv5}.  It
was selected from the largest AOM-PLS screening denominator and then compared
in the main text with the pre-registered linear references.  Against
PLS-default, it reaches a median RMSEP ratio of 0.985 on $N=52$ datasets with
33/52 wins.  Against the completed PLS-HPO subset, it is essentially tied:
median ratio 1.002 on $N=32$, 15/32 wins.

This is the intended interpretation.  Full external preprocessing-HPO can
recover the benefit of careful preprocessing, but the gain is obtained by
repeatedly fitting the linear calibration under a much larger search budget.
AOM-PLS makes the strict-linear part of the same decision inside one
coefficient-bearing calibration object.

\subsection{Ridge regression}

The plain Ridge AOM baseline is \texttt{AOMRidge-global-compact-none}.  It
improves over default Ridge with a median RMSEP ratio of 0.974 on $N=52$
datasets, 41/52 wins and Holm-adjusted $p=0.002$.  On the completed Ridge-HPO
overlap, it remains close to the grid baseline: median ratio 0.978 on
$N=34$, 21/34 wins.

The selected Ridge variant is \texttt{AOMRidge-Blender-headline-spxy3}.  It
improves strongly over default Ridge: median RMSEP ratio 0.913 on $N=52$
datasets with 44/52 wins and Holm-adjusted $p=2.9\times10^{-6}$.  It also
improves over the completed Ridge-HPO subset, with median ratio 0.956 on
$N=34$, 27/34 wins and $p=0.011$.

The Ridge result supports the operator-kernel view.  In Ridge, preprocessing
changes the regularized sample geometry.  Blender is the best main selector
because it combines strong operator geometries through internal validation
while preserving a single deployable Ridge model.

