\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_results.pdf}
  \caption{Paired regression summary for the plain compact-bank AOM baselines
  and the selected AOM-PLS and AOM-Ridge variants.}
  \label{fig:results}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Classification}

The selected classification variant is
\texttt{AOM-PLS-DA-global-simpls-covariance}.  On the paired classification
cohort, it improves balanced accuracy by a median 0.159 on $N=13$ datasets,
with 12/13 wins and Holm-adjusted $p=0.007$.  This secondary result confirms
that the covariance-space operator selection used for regression transfers
to PLS-DA when the response encoding and scoring metric are changed.

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Main classification result.  Positive balanced-accuracy
  differences favour the operator-adaptive classifier.}
  \label{tab:classification_main}
  \small
  \input{tables/table_classification_main.tex}
\end{table}

\subsection{Time budget}

The practical advantage is compute.  PLS-HPO has a median total time of
984.90 s per completed run and an observed successful-row total of 162.4 h.
The plain \texttt{AOM-compact-cv5} branch has a median total time of 1.76 s,
and the selected AOM-PLS branch has a median total time of 1.94 s and an
observed total of 0.2 h.  On median total time, the selected AOM-PLS branch is
about 508 times faster than PLS-HPO.  In candidate evaluations, the contrast
is 3000 trial fits per PLS-HPO dataset/seed versus 45 compact-bank CV
evaluations for AOM-PLS.

Ridge is different because the selected Blender selector is heavier.  It
still improves over Ridge-HPO on the completed overlap, and its median total
time is 960.71 s compared with 2024.55 s for Ridge-HPO.  The plain
\texttt{AOMRidge-global-compact-none} baseline is much lighter, with median
fit time 21.60 s.  The result is not that every AOM selector is instant; it is
that strict-linear preprocessing can be represented as a model geometry rather
than as a blind external pipeline loop.

