\subsection{AOM-PLS solvers and coefficient recovery}

A standard PLS coefficient matrix can be written as
\begin{equation}
  \B=\mathbf W(\mathbf P\transpose\mathbf W)^+\mathbf Q\transpose ,
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf W$ are weights, $\mathbf P$ are spectral loadings,
$\mathbf Q$ are response loadings and $+$ denotes the Moore--Penrose inverse.
In AOM-PLS, a component may be selected in a transformed space.  If the
transformed-space direction is $r_a$ under operator $\A_b$, its
original-wavelength direction is
\begin{equation}
  z_a=\A_b\transpose r_a .
\end{equation}
With $\Z=[z_1,\ldots,z_k]$ and scores $\T=\X\Z$, the recovered coefficient
matrix is
\begin{equation}
  \B=\Z(\mathbf P\transpose\Z)^+\mathbf Q\transpose .
  \label{eq:aompls_coef}
\end{equation}
The fitted model is therefore still one linear calibration on the original
wavelength grid.

Two solver views are implemented.  The NIPALS-adjoint solver applies operator
adjoints during iterative score extraction.  The SIMPLS-covariance solver
uses Equation~\ref{eq:cov_identity} directly in covariance space.  The main
plain baseline is \texttt{AOM-compact-cv5}: the compact nine-operator bank
with internal five-fold selection and no fitted ASLS branch.  The selected
regression result uses the same compact CV5 branch after ASLS is fitted
fold-locally.  The default bank, SIMPLS-only variants, and other non-selected
AOM variants are reported in the supplementary material.

The compact bank was chosen from the selector diagnostics, not by adding an
unbounded operator catalogue.  In the refreshed diagnostics, the three most
frequent strict operators
(\texttt{sg\_smooth\_w21\_p3}, \texttt{sg\_d1\_w21\_p3} and
\texttt{sg\_d1\_w11\_p2}) account for 54.7\% of compact-bank component
selections, while identity remains selected on 8.7\% of components.  The
operator bank is therefore diverse enough to adapt, but small enough to keep
the selection problem stable.

\subsection{AOM-Ridge operator kernels}

For centered spectra $\X_c$ and response $\Y_c$, Ridge regression can be
solved in the dual.  Operator $\A_b$ induces the kernel
\begin{equation}
  \K_b=\X_c\A_b\transpose\A_b\X_c\transpose .
  \label{eq:ridge_kernel}
\end{equation}
For regularization $\alpha>0$,
\begin{equation}
  \mathbf C=(\K_b+\alpha\mathbf I_n)^{-1}\Y_c,
  \qquad
  \bm\beta_b=\A_b\transpose\A_b\X_c\transpose\mathbf C .
  \label{eq:ridge_beta}
\end{equation}
Weighted operator mixtures are obtained by replacing $\K_b$ with
\begin{equation}
  \K=\sum_b s_b^2\X_c\A_b\transpose\A_b\X_c\transpose,
\end{equation}
which gives an original-space coefficient matrix through the matching
operator metric
$\sum_b s_b^2\A_b\transpose\A_b$.

The plain Ridge baseline is \texttt{AOMRidge-global-compact-none}: one global
selection over the compact strict-linear bank, without Blender mixing or local
neighbourhood routing.  The selected Ridge result uses the Blender selector
(\texttt{AOMRidge-Blender-headline-spxy3}).  It combines several strong
operator geometries through internal validation rather than committing to one
noisy winner.  GCV, local-neighbourhood and additional global compact
selectors are mathematically described in the supplement.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_math.pdf}
  \caption{AOM mathematical structure.  AOM-PLS screens operators through
  cross-covariances; AOM-Ridge screens operator-induced kernels.  Both recover
  coefficients on the original wavelength grid.}
  \label{fig:math}
\end{figure}

\section{Datasets and protocol}
\label{sec:datasets}

\subsection{Simulation checks}

The implementation checks use explicit-vs-operator simulations for the PLS
covariance identity and the Ridge kernel identity.  These checks verify that
for fixed folds and fixed strict operators, the folded computation matches
the explicit transformed computation used by an external search.

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Dataset shape summary for the AOM cohort.  Here $n$ is the total
  calibration plus external-test sample count, $p$ is the number of spectral
  variables, $C$ is the number of classes and $I$ is the largest-class share
  for classification rows.}
  \label{tab:dataset_statistics}
  \small
  \input{tables/table_dataset_statistics.tex}
\end{table}

Whenever a source dataset already defined an external split, that split was
preserved.  Otherwise deterministic SPXY was used for regression, with the
stratified variant used for classification when class labels were present
\citep{kennard1969computer,galvao2005method}.  Test sets were kept outside
preprocessing, operator, component and regularization selection.  No explicit
outlier removal was applied during cohort construction: calibration and test
samples were kept as supplied by the source datasets.

\subsection{Benchmark protocol}

The empirical benchmark uses every available AOM data run listed in the review
workspace.  The main AOM-PLS comparison has 53 non-QUARTZ paired datasets for
variant screening and 52 against the default-CV PLS reference.  The main
AOM-Ridge comparison has 52 paired datasets against default Ridge.  Full
cartesian preprocessing-HPO completed for 36 PLS datasets and 35 Ridge
datasets across three seeds.  The classification cohort contains 16 included
AOM-PLS-DA rows, with 13 paired datasets for the selected classifier.

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Cohort denominators and data-quality rules used in the main
  manuscript.  Every comparison below is stated on its own paired
  denominator.}
  \label{tab:benchmark_diversity}
  \small
  \input{tables/table_benchmark_diversity.tex}
\end{table}

All selection steps are internal to the calibration set.  RMSEP is the primary
regression metric.  Because response scales differ across traits, aggregate
regression results use paired RMSEP ratios.  Classification uses balanced
accuracy.  Paired tests use one-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank tests with Holm
correction within the displayed family.

The cartesian-HPO baselines are intentionally strong and expensive.  They
enumerate 600 preprocessing combinations.  PLS-HPO uses five trials per
combination; Ridge-HPO uses ten trials per combination.  These baselines were
evaluated on the subset of datasets that completed within the compute budget;
the LUCAS\_SOC rows did not complete, and two FUSARIUM rows failed estimator
checks because the response contained NaN values.

